# Include this in the accessible.project-nexus.ie vhost, and in each community's
# own accessible-domain vhost.
#
# 🔴 NOT YET INSTALLED ANYWHERE. Installing this file is cutover step 2 and needs
# explicit authorisation. See docs/ACCESSIBLE-FRONTEND-TAKEOVER.md.
#
# ProxyPreserveHost is LOAD-BEARING here, more than for the other two vhosts:
# web-uk resolves a community from the Host header, so stripping it would make
# every slug-less custom-domain request resolve to the wrong community or none.

ProxyPreserveHost On
ProxyPass /.well-known/acme-challenge/ !

# 🔴 Why the fallback exists, and why it must stay.
#
# NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT is only defined by the deploy script when web-uk is actually
# deployed (NEXUS_DEPLOY_WEBUK=1). Apache treats an undefined ${VAR} in a
# ProxyPass target as a literal string, which fails `apachectl configtest`.
#
# Without the <IfDefine !...> arm below, a ROLLBACK to any release predating
# web-uk would rewrite the routes file without NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT, this include
# would then reference an undefined variable, configtest would fail — and the
# deploy script treats a failed configtest as a reason to abort and restore.
# So the rollback would abort itself, at exactly the moment it is most needed.
#
# 🔴 WHAT THE FALLBACK ARM IS FOR, CORRECTED 2026-08-14.
#
# It used to serve the accessible hostname from the Blade accessible frontend, and
# that really was the right behaviour when rolling back to a release predating
# web-uk. Blade has now been DELETED from the codebase, so the fallback no longer
# reaches a working frontend — Laravel has no accessible routes and members would
# get errors.
#
# The arm is deliberately KEPT anyway, and it is important to understand why: its
# job is to keep `${NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT}` from being an undefined variable. Without it
# a rollback to a pre-web-uk release fails `apachectl configtest`, and the deploy
# script treats a failed configtest as a reason to abort and restore — so the
# rollback would abort itself at the worst possible moment.
#
# So: this arm exists to keep configtest passing, NOT to provide a working site.
# If it ever takes effect, the accessible hostnames are down and the fix is a
# deploy that includes web-uk (`--with-webuk`).
<IfDefine NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT>
    ProxyPass / http://127.0.0.1:${NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT}/ retry=0
    ProxyPassReverse / http://127.0.0.1:${NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT}/
</IfDefine>
<IfDefine !NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT>
    # 🔴 NOT a working accessible frontend since 2026-08-14 — see the note above.
    # Present only so ${NEXUS_WEBUK_PORT} is never undefined during a rollback.
    ProxyPass / http://127.0.0.1:${NEXUS_API_PORT}/ retry=0
    ProxyPassReverse / http://127.0.0.1:${NEXUS_API_PORT}/
</IfDefine>

# 🔴 VERIFY THIS ON THE REAL SERVER BEFORE RELYING ON IT.
#
# The whole cheap-rollback story rests on Define/<IfDefine> interacting the way
# this file assumes, and that has NOT been confirmed against the production
# Apache build. Confirm both arms before cutover:
#
#   # with web-uk deployed — expect the webuk arm and a clean configtest
#   sudo apachectl configtest
#   # then remove the Define line from the routes file and re-test — expect the
#   # PHP arm and a clean configtest, NOT a failure
#   sudo apachectl configtest
#
# If <IfDefine> does not behave as assumed, fix it here BEFORE any cutover
# rather than discovering it during a rollback.
